    "lastCommit": {
      "id": "1a3dc7c94b72b1e01e67a5dc77293a95b1ac0de8",
      "message": "OTA-1813 \u2014 the tap says how far it got\n\nOBSERVATIONAL TELEMETRY ONLY. This does NOT repair the gameplay freeze and does\nNOT claim a cause for it. Apple-freeze causation remains UNKNOWN.\n\nTHE QUESTION IT ANSWERS. When the player reports that the screen has stopped\ntaking touch, three different faults produce the same symptom: the finger never\nreached the app's own tree, it reached a handler and was refused, or it reached\na handler and the work that handler scheduled never ran. They need three\ndifferent repairs and the record could not tell them apart \u2014 `ui: tap` is\nstamped when the JS handler RUNS, so a touch that never reached one leaves no\ntrace at all.\n\nTHE CHAIN, every link an observer that changes nothing:\n  T0 root      ExplorationScreen's onStartShouldSetResponderCapture, RETURNING\n               FALSE \u2014 it asks the capture-phase question and never becomes the\n               responder, so no control loses a gesture.\n  M0 modal     the same observer on SearchModal / GatherModal / ClimbModal.\n               PROVEN NECESSARY, not assumed: all three are presented by a\n               native <Modal>, whose content is hosted outside the Exploration\n               tree, so T0 cannot see a touch that lands there.\n  T1 in        each instrumented control's own onPressIn.\n  T2 enter     the first statement of the completed handler.\n  T3 admit/reject   what the handler DECIDED.\n  T4/T5 dispatch/done   the verb went out; the handler returned.\n  armed/fired  submitAfterSheetSettles defers work by 400ms. An `armed` with no\n               `fired` is the difference between a tap never heard and a tap\n               heard and then dropped.\n\nA REJECTION IS ONLY WRITTEN WHERE SOURCE PROVES A REAL REFUSAL \u2014 a branch that\nbuzzes and returns without reaching a verb. A `disabled` Pressable that\ncorrectly never runs its handler is NOT recorded as a refusal, and neither is an\nout-of-range travel tap, which vibrates and then PROCEEDS (OTA-1591). An\ninstrument that invents a refusal would answer the question wrong in the\nconfident direction; the suite counts them so it cannot.\n\ntapClock IS REUSED, NOT EXTENDED, AND IS LEFT UNTOUCHED. It keeps ONE mutable\nslot and consumes it \u2014 right for its `\u23f1+Nms` suffix, useless for correlation,\nsince two fingers would share a slot. touchPath keeps its own bounded claim\nqueue: newest-first, claimed once, age-bounded, so a handler running after a\nstall says `orphan` instead of adopting an older touch's identity. Only\n`touchLateMs` is borrowed, purely to compute a number.\n\nPERSISTED BECAUSE THE FREEZE ENDS IN A FORCE-CLOSE \u2014 the boot that can send a\nreport is never the boot that froze. ONE write in flight ever: a leading\nsnapshot, then dirty-marking, then a single coalesced trailing write. No\nper-stage writes, no parallel writes, no recurring timer, no retry loop, every\nfailure swallowed. The prior boot's tail is hydrated once at start and printed\nin the report header above this boot's.\n\nSELF-RISK GUARDRAILS HELD. Nothing added serialises player actions, debounces a\ncontrol, adds a mutex, changes gameplay admission, claims a responder, remounts\na presentation, awaits AsyncStorage on the touch path, updates React state or\nwakes a store subscriber from T0/T1, touches native code, or creates an\nunbounded allocation. Ring, claim queue and string fields are all bounded by\nconstruction; the clipped fields are a privacy property as much as a size one.\n\nTWO PROTECTED PINS RETARGETED, NEITHER SOFTENED, BOTH DOCUMENTED IN PLACE:\n  ota1695 counted the literal prop `onPressIn={noteTouchDown}`; T1 required a\n  composed handler, so the CALL is pinned instead \u2014 which also newly proves\n  tapClock is fed FIRST. ota1806 pinned a one-line blocked branch and the same\n  prop on TravelBtn; the branch is now pinned as the behaviour its own title\n  names (buzz, nudge, return before the verb). OTA-1497's deferral statement is\n  untouched byte for byte \u2014 `after` is composed before the timeout is scheduled.\n\nVERIFICATION. 22/22 check gates green. typecheck:ci clean; typecheck:tests at\nbaseline 201 (NOT raised); lint clean. New suite ota1813TheTapSaysHowFarItGot\n39/39 over eleven sections. Four negative controls each went red on a reversed\nproduction condition and green again \u2014 T0 returning true, a modal observer\nremoved, the `fired` half dropped, and a fabricated out-of-range rejection \u2014\nand none remains in the tree. Full fast surface 1357/1357 suites, 14,361 tests,\nre-run on this exact tree.\n\nWHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. No test here can present a native iOS modal or reproduce a\nfreeze, so nothing in this repository shows where a touch actually dies. The\ninstrument's answer requires a DEVICE REPRODUCTION to be worth anything.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01LGFtzR6AQJbu4XdR28y3fX",
